    "turn_text": "people in New Hampshire are really cool. I'd say get in the game. This is a problem facing your generation. You have to have a voice in the decision. Welcome to Facing the Future, brought to you by the Conquered Coalition on WKXL, New Hampshire's talk radio station. I'm your host, Bob Bixby. Each week, we take a nonpartisan dive into topics related to the federal budget, the economy, and how it all affects our nation's future. Starting next week, you can hear Facing the Future at the same time, but on a new day. We're moving to Thursdays at 3 p.m. right here on WKXL. This week, we'll look at the future of Social Security with Reed Ribble, who served in Congress as a Republican representing the 8th District of Wisconsin from 2011 to 2017. In 2016, Ribble released the Save Our Social Security Act, yes, SOS. with a bipartisan group of cosponsors and we'll talk to him about that and also about the busy agenda Congress is facing in 2025 when several tax cuts are scheduled to expire and the debt limit springs back to life. Conquered coalition policy director Tory Gorman and chief economist Steve Robinson joined the conversation. Well, just as a little background on our guest during his tenure in Congress, Reed Ribbles served on several important committees. He was on the Agriculture Committee, Transportation and Infrastructure, Budget and Finance, and Foreign Affairs Committees. That's quite a portfolio. Before running for Congress, Ribble was president of his own roofing company in Wisconsin for more than 30 years. And after his congressional tenure, he became CEO of the roofers contractors association. He retired from that position in 2022 and is currently the first practitioner in residence at the University of Wisconsin's Green Bay political science program. And we might talk to him a little bit about that. So quite a background for this. Mr. Congressman, so welcome, welcome Steve Tory to Facing the Future. Congressman, our main subject is Social Security today, and you introduce the bipartisan Social Security Reform Bill, and before we get into the details, I recall a very hot July day in 2016. when I joined you and your co-sponsors of the Save Our Social Security Act outside the Capitol in the beating sun to introduce the bill. And I want to begin today with what I said on that occasion on the bill. I said, this bill is distinguished by courageous common sense and the recognition that any Social Security reform will require political compromise. Unlike many in Washington, the co-sponsors of this legislation have taken seriously the warnings from the Social Security trustees that the system is not on a sustainable course. Sadly, Congressman Ribble, you retired. Nobody's really picked up the mantle. That seems to be the case. So, you know, why did a Congressman from Consum feel it was important to get into the details and write his own Social Security Reform Plan?",
