  {
    "episode_id": 65119,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, when I went into it, I decided that the only way you can have any hope of having a full fix, a 100% fix for a 100% problem is how I worded it, would be to have enough things in that that both sides would hate. And so I thought, okay, what is it that Republicans and conservatives typically hate? Well, they hate revenue increases, tax increases. So I said, what is the most fair way to adjust the revenue? And it was by resetting payroll tax at its historic norms of capturing 90% of all payrolls. At the time in 2016, it was capturing about 80%. And so today's worker is actually paying less than Social Security tax than what our parents did and past generation did. And so we needed to address the revenue side and I got the obligatory reaction from my Republican colleagues. But then I also said we also need to address reality. Reality is that Americans are not just living longer, they are working longer and they are healthier into older age. And we also need to recognize that there are fewer workers contributing to pay for this current generation of seniors. So we needed to adjust the age. And so I would tweak the age up by two years over a 12 year period. And then I also address the means testing a bit by saying very wealthy people are earning over 400,000 a year in the retirement. We're going to take that fourth Ben point down on the primary insurance and reduce that. I actually added money for the very agent because the data showed that once, particularly, a widow would reach age 90 that exhausted their personal savings and some of them were living on $700 or $800 a month. And so we were actually forcing people in the poverty that were in the program. And so I adjusted the minimum benefit to 125% of poverty. And then I adjusted the COLA to go to a chain CPI from CPIW. Those things in their collective way back in 2016 would have made Social Security actually balanced for 75 years. And so it was a tough lift though because I felt like a dog chase in my tail bob because by the time I got something scored it was already behind. And so I had to over correct to fix the problem and today these reforms wouldn't go far enough.",
