  {
    "episode_id": 65119,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Well, mathematics drove things first, but I did go back to President Reagan's reform back in the early 1980s, where he reset the payroll tax, and he was a Republican. And so he proved it could be done if he had presidential leadership. And so I looked at some of those reforms. He raised the age. You know, so some of those things were there, but they were inadequate to the task today. But I also had a philosophical problem where I didn't want to over tax employment in the country. And so I could have fixed some of the revenue by saying, well, that's raised at actual tax rate. That would have passed more burden on to lower income middle class Americans. And so I didn't want to do that because that can actually, by raising the cost of employment, hurt employment opportunities for Americans. And so, I mean, there were all kinds of influences at the time that were coming in, but when it was all said and done, it came down to arithmetic. In there, I created a big spreadsheet that had all these sensitivities like what happens if immigration policy changes? What happens if the birth rate or death rate change? And every time I would make an adjustment on one of the changes, I would get a different number out of the bottom of my spreadsheet. So it was a big task.",
