  {
    "episode_id": 65119,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well, yeah, I would agree that that's the case. Unless you choose to do what sadly members of Congress and both parties are choosing to do right now, and that is to just reduce benefits by 25% in 2033 to 34. when the trust fund in essence goes, resets back to zero, and we're just paying off, we're paying out whatever payroll taxes come in. And so, but those two elements have to be part of it, or you can't get there. And even John Larson, who's got a, Congressman Larson's got a bill, his Social Security 2300 or whatever it is, 2100, he does everything on the payroll tax side, but that only extends the life of the program for 20 years. So, you know, it's about a 40% fix for a 100% problem. And there's nothing in there with which to negotiate with the other side. And as you probably could recognize Tory, that if you don't have something to trade off in a big comprehensive bill, you don't really get anything.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
