  {
    "episode_id": 65119,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "So, Congressman, I noticed one element missing from your proposal that used to be sort of a standard element in Social Security Reform Proposals, and that is some idea of related to personal accounts. When I was on the Hill back in the late 90s and then again in the mid-2000s, I worked on a couple of Social Security Reform Proposals And one of the big items, particularly among Republican members, was the notion that you would use part of the payroll tax, have workers invest in sort of their personal account, sort of like an IRA or 401K, and that money would be used to supplement their Social Security benefit. And that became a real point of contention between Republicans and Democrats, where the Republicans would insist on what they called a carve-out, meaning that they would take the existing payroll tax and put it in account. And some of the Democrats who were a little more receptive to personal accounts would say, well, no, no, no, we don't want to carve out existing payroll taxes, but we'll do add on account. So if you put additional money into the system, that would be added into the personal account, and that would supplement your Social Security. And again, this was a little before your proposal came along. So I guess my question would be, did you have those discussions with some of your colleagues and did you consider a personal account element and why did that not make it into your proposal?",
