    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, thanks Steve. We had a lot of conversations about it. The problem was we're too far underwater. And so if you divert payroll taxes to personal accounts, all you do is blow up the debt. And it exacerbates the problem rather than corrects it. And in between the time that you guys were working on it in today, there's been a lot of reforms on tax-affert accounts, Roth IRAs, Roth 401Ks, 401Ks, 403Bs, there's a lot of different ways for the American people to begin to both attacks deferred or even almost non-tax methods to set aside money for their own retirement. And so as those programs became more popular and more companies were embracing and providing those alternatives for their employees. The idea of personal accounts became less appealing to me when added to the fact that diverting funds from the Social Security payroll tax in an environment where we were already upside down would just double down on the national debt. And it really wouldn't save us anything. And so we ended up eliminating it. And with that, we eliminated the, what I often would tell people if something is not politically feasible, it's not feasible rule. I didn't believe it was politically feasible, and so I chose not to put it in",
