    "turn_text": "was exactly like that. I was going to members offices and sitting down in the face of face because I had a whole list of members that I had on video or audio tape saying that the deficit was a problem. The debt was a problem. And then at some point we have to fix Social Security. And so I went to those guys first and said, you yourself are campaigning on this. Just think of how powerful this could be if you would go back home and say, I have now got a bipartisan plan. to secure not just your future but every single americans retirement between now and the next seventy five years and that they did exactly what you said they docked under the mattress for the most part but i had a lot of resistance from the speaker and a lot of resistance from the ways and means committee. Because we were controlled by Republicans at the time. Paul Ryan was kind of the budget walk guy in the Congress. He didn't like the plan because his plan was all basically changing benefits because he wouldn't add any revenue. And it began to dawn on me that the Republican plan when I was in Congress was to let it just run out. That was their plan. And because then do it automatically get their best wishes were basically to get it to bounce by cutting benefits. And I had at the time a 90 year old mother-in-law who was a widow for 20 years who had no savings whatsoever and was living on $770 a month. And so I said, we've got a problem here. We've got to we've got to stop and think differently about these programs and come up with a bipartisan solution for it. Troy.",
