    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "You may recall last year during the State of the Union, President Biden had a line where he got all the members to stand up for seniors, and what he implied in that context was that the members of Congress were, along with himself, were promising not to cut Social Security or Medicare benefits. You mentioned earlier about, so the Republicans maybe thought that the do-nothing plan would lead to the automatic benefit cuts. The do-nothing plan from a Democrat perspective might also lead to a different result, and that would be that once the trust fund is about to go broke, instead of letting benefits cut, they would simply For lack of a better phrase, they would simply stuff the trust fund with IOUs, meaning they would allow general revenue to be borrowed and transferred into the trust fund. So you could, in a sense, avoid the benefit cuts, avoid a payroll tax cut simply by using general revenue, which of course we don't have, which means we would have to borrow it. So, you know, is this notion that we're going to somehow do nothing on social security until we reach the precipice of trust fund and solvency, and each side is hoping their preferred outcome will occur, that the benefits will either be cut, or that general revenue will be used to avoid that. I mean, what's your sense of simply waiting this out and doing nothing? What are the politics and what's the implications of that?",
