  {
    "episode_id": 65119,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "Well, there isn't any general revenue. So the reality is we're running deficits now that are nearly $2 trillion a year. You want to jump those to $3 trillion a year, sure. But there is no general revenue. And so if they go to that place, and this is likely to be what happens, as both President Biden and former president trump the two leading nominees for the presidency this fall both of them said they're not going to reform social security they both said we're not going to touch it so in essence they both admitted to themselves and to their voters that the government's going to come in and bail out the program at some point but not under their watch and so that's not leadership that's cowardice and so um and i know that the democrats republicans who support their candidates don't want me to call call it that but that's what's going on And you cannot plug a hole with nothing. And so what they're doing is plugging, they would be plugging the hole with borrowed money. and what would be a much better solution for the next several generations. And by the way, President Obama was willing to address this problem. And I think part of it is because he was so much younger. And if there's ever a reason that you can point to that we need to move to the next generation of presidential leadership, it's this very topic. because the old guys want to leave things alone as if it's all being taken care of in the next generation. It's the next generation's problem. Well, I actually love the next generation because it consists of my two sons and my seven grandsons. And so it's very, very important for us to deal with this in a pragmatic thoughtful manner that can protect seniors and protect workers. And one of the things that not been mentioned in all this discussion is the role that immigration reform plays into this. We have, I don't know how many million of workers wanted to get into this country. I just gave a recent speech called, is the American Dream Dead? And my closing slide was a picture of a half a million people trying to break into the United States at the southern border to capture their own version of it. So no, the American Dream is not dead, but we have to think differently when you got declining birth rates. and not enough workers. One of the fixes here is to have more people than the workforce. We need to change our immigration law to encourage more people to work and to employ those people. And here's the data point, Bob. Every first world country in north of the equator without exception has birth rates below replacement level. So there will be a global competition for labor coming here in the next decade or so, and the United States better get prepared because it's all coming from the southern hemisphere, either South America or Africa, and we better be prepared to tap into that workforce, or we're going to be in a world of hurt.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
