  {
    "episode_id": 65119,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, okay. The first one is if the government would just repay the money they've stolen, the whole idea that the government is stolen this money. They've invested the money into bonds that are paying interest back into the program. What are they supposed to do with it? Stick it under a mattress or into a closet someplace where it doesn't earn any type of interest. You can't invest it into the stock market, which might end up being one of the suggested alternatives going forward because you need to get better returns. But without regard, the government has not stolen this money. They are paying interest on the money and they utilize it as it comes in. And I would say the second one is, we just need a tax to reach more. If we would just tax to reach more, we could fix this whole problem. And there aren't enough rich people to fix this problem. And by the way, Congress wants to tax to fix every single problem. At some point this golden egg is going to be fried and there's no money left to tap into. And so this is a program that was designed to have the rich involved in it and have the poor involved in it so that you wouldn't have people with lots and lots of money and influence take the program down. The idea that we're just going to tax the rich more does not solve this problem.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The government has not stolen the money in question.",
