    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "I don't know that there's time for a small ball approach anymore, Tori. I think there was a period of time that you could have done more of a single step approach on it. I think the program is in such trouble right now financially that it's going to require something bigger. I would be wide open to any type of reform that would move the needle a little bit, although typically a small ball reform is never bipartisan. And so then it becomes, it gets into my catch 22, if something's not politically feasible, it's not feasible. And so unless one party controls both chambers of Congress and the White House and then they do something via budget reconciliation, I know we're kind of getting in the weeds here, all that does is get half of the country angry because they got left out of the equation. And so I think in something for a big issue like this, big issue like immigration reform, Medicare reform These things have to be done in a broader reform vehicle so that you've got something on the table to begin to negotiate with and trade. And that's one of the reasons you don't see a lot of little tweaks to the program. There are some, and you'll see a lot of little tweaks to the program. But if they were to try to tweak one thing, might be able to get done. It would probably be with addressing the call of the cost of living adjustment. You might be able to change it off a CPIW to some other formula that might be what offers some savings and get eight or nine or ten years of benefit from. But even that, if it results in a net decrease in cost and living adjustments. You'll have one party jump all over it, so I'm not sure that it's possible to do it in a small ball way.",
