    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "So you mentioned earlier that one of the keys to reform was to try to convince the political parties, the political arms of the parties to sign a non-aggression pact. What are the prospects for that though? There's also often the comment that politicians would rather have an issue than a solution. that it's politically more advantageous to beat up your opponent or attack your opponent than it is to put forward bipartisan common sense proposals. And given the political divide, I mean, most incumbents are more likely to be facing a primary opponent than they are a competitive opponent from the other party in the general election. So if each party, the Republicans like the no tax pledge or whatever, the Republicans are worried if they propose a payroll tax change, they'll get a primary opponent and the Democrats are afraid if they propose a benefit change, they'll get a primary opponent. So both parties are sort of locked in by the extreme elements of their own party to find some common ground. I mean, is there a way around that?",
