    "turn_text": "Yeah, it's both. But let's take them kind of one at a time and try to go really fast on the expiring tax cuts. Congress doesn't have to do anything. They can just ignore it. And those tax cuts go away. They just expire and they're done. So they don't really have to take any action on if they choose not to. That's up to them. And they'll be under pressure to do it, obviously. When it comes to dealing with the debt limit, The debt limits is phony as the federal budget. Listen, there is no such thing as a debt limit. They can create a debt limit and say, we're not going to go above this debt limit, but they're spending above the debt limit today. And so they're going to have to raise the borrowing authority as they have done 100% of the time. And so a limit that gets broken 100% of the time is no limit at all. If they really wanted to be common sensical, they would put the debt limit vote with any appropriation that goes above the level so that they'd have to be raising that debt limit all the time. And that would be right in front of the American people. And that would be the best thing to apply public pressure on them to restrain unnecessary spending. And then when it comes to Social Security, they do have a little bit more time. then they do with Medicare, and I think Medicare becomes the initial crisis that they're going to have to deal with. And at the end of the day, and I'm saying this as a Republican, the reality is, going back to my original statement, if it's not politically feasible, it's not feasible. The reality is, on healthcare, this country is headed toward a single-payer system, and you're not going to be able to avoid it. You can delay it, but that's where this is headed.",
